1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,320
Hi, Mom! I'm home!

2
00:00:09,320 --> 00:00:11,920
What you cook in there? It smells amazing!

3
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:15,360
It's your birthday, remember?

4
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:18,660
I made all the recipes you love.

5
00:00:19,340 --> 00:00:20,760
Wow! You made them all?

6
00:00:21,020 --> 00:00:22,360
Every single dish.

7
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:25,680
See? I whipped up all of your favorites.

8
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:28,460
Fresh from the bells of the dungeon!

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,260
Good morning. You okay?

10
00:00:35,580 --> 00:00:37,220
I had a nightmare.

11
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:42,000
That's what I smelled in my nightmare!

12
00:00:42,440 --> 00:00:43,840
Can you guys smell that, too?

13
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:46,860
Yep. The party across the way is making breakfast.

14
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:48,840
They're having salted pork?

15
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:50,420
On buns?

16
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:52,060
It's not fair!

17
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:53,180
It's horrible.

18
00:00:53,940 --> 00:00:55,640
Breaks my heart to see it.

19
00:00:55,640 --> 00:01:00,340
These days, the diet of young adventurers is a travesty.

20
00:01:00,820 --> 00:01:03,220
They only eat bread, dried meats, and wine.

21
00:01:03,740 --> 00:01:08,140
Look, I get it. Exploring dungeons takes stamina, which you build, by eating fatty meat.

22
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:09,600
But fat ain't enough.

23
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:12,920
Malnutrition is the real monster here.

24
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:20,000
Now that I said my piece, I feel like a total hypocrite, because we have yet to eat a balanced meal.

25
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:24,800
Elf girl, you know why you were drooling over that salted pork yonder?

26
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:28,380
It's because your body's craving fat, that's why.

27
00:01:28,580 --> 00:01:30,280
It's because I don't want to eat monster!

28
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:33,220
Today, we'll hunt monsters that are rich and fat.

29
00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:36,180
Honestly, it'd be nice if we could find one that laid eggs, too.

30
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:39,600
Eggs are filling, nutritious, the perfect food.

31
00:01:40,020 --> 00:01:42,300
So, if you spot any eggs around, grab them.

32
00:01:43,060 --> 00:01:44,820
You thinking what I'm thinking?

33
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:46,000
I am.

34
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:47,120
Just say it.

35
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:49,000
The body of a chicken.

36
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:51,220
The tail of a snake.

37
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:54,560
With spurs, sharp as knives, and loaded with venom.

38
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:57,720
Basilisk, the king of snakes!

39
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:00,160
All right, a basilisk.

40
00:02:00,820 --> 00:02:01,900
Isn't that great, Marcel?

41
00:02:01,900 --> 00:02:03,640
You'll get to eat chicken meat.

42
00:02:05,220 --> 00:02:06,140
Is it, though?

43
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:08,380
Can you call it a chicken when it's half chicken?

44
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:10,240
Man, what a cool concept.

45
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:13,600
I always loved monsters that were a mix of more than one species.

46
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:18,160
Something about mashing two species together elevates the appeal of each one.

47
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:22,440
The basilisk makes you appreciate the things about a plain old chicken that make it special.

48
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:24,960
A simple combo, but it really makes you think.

49
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:28,660
By the way, here's a fact about the cockatrice and basilisk.

50
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:30,380
Same family, different species.

51
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:34,000
It's been my dream to do a taste test between the two.

52
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:35,920
You got some weird dreams.

53
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:39,280
There should be a basilisk nest here.

54
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:42,680
There's one.

55
00:02:43,380 --> 00:02:45,140
The eggs look freshly laid, too.

56
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:48,540
Let's bag them up before the folks come home.

57
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:50,460
Are they supposed to be that long?

58
00:02:50,460 --> 00:02:51,180
Here, take it.

59
00:02:51,820 --> 00:02:53,760
But are they supposed to be this squishy?

60
00:02:54,580 --> 00:02:56,280
The cry of the basilisk.

61
00:02:56,540 --> 00:02:57,420
Come on, Senshi.

62
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:00,160
I hope you wrapped yours in something soft like I did.

63
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:04,660
What's the party with the grill?

64
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:34,480
Hmm, bad escape technique.

65
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:36,720
She's just asking to be kicked in the back.

66
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:38,420
Would you just hurry up and save her?

67
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:49,760
Spread your arms and legs out wide to look huge.

68
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:51,700
Then do a crazy yell to sound threatening.

69
00:03:52,160 --> 00:03:53,720
It'll freak the thing out and keep it back.

70
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:59,420
How about we pretend we don't know him?

71
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:00,600
Stop!

72
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:11,700
The basilisk has another head on its tail, so it seems like it has no blind spots.

73
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:13,200
But it's still one body.

74
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:17,880
So if you attack both heads at the same time, you'll split its tension in two directions.

75
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:20,940
Confusing the whole body.

76
00:04:25,340 --> 00:04:26,320
How is he?

77
00:04:27,060 --> 00:04:28,260
Did he get poisoned?

78
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:29,200
Yes.

79
00:04:29,660 --> 00:04:30,140
Laios!

80
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:32,380
Senshi says he has an antidote.

81
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:33,640
Let's have it, then.

82
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:37,040
Actually, I was planning to use it for lunch today.

83
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:37,940
Huh?

84
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:39,860
He's dying!

85
00:04:40,140 --> 00:04:41,120
Can't you see that?

86
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:45,040
Yeah, well, it's better to take one of these antidotes if it's cooked into something.

87
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:46,540
Then hurry up and cook something!

88
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:49,780
First, you gotta chop off the tail and feet.

89
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:52,860
Parboil it for a sec, then pluck the feathers.

90
00:04:54,340 --> 00:04:54,820
Whoa!

91
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:56,880
Looks like normal chicken.

92
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:00,280
Next, you gotta pull out the guts and rub in some spices.

93
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:04,180
Chop some vegetables up and cram them in with your medicinal herbs.

94
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:05,800
Then tie up the cut.

95
00:05:06,360 --> 00:05:10,020
Last, jam a stick through the sucker and give it a good slow roasting.

96
00:05:11,060 --> 00:05:12,020
And it's ready.

97
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:13,120
Hurry up!

98
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:13,800
Give him some!

99
00:05:16,540 --> 00:05:21,760
It's so juicy and delicious.

100
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:23,860
Sorry for the wait.

101
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:26,180
Here, have some, too.

102
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:27,480
I can't yet!

103
00:05:27,740 --> 00:05:29,340
I should cast a healing spell, too.

104
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:33,620
Oh, my gosh.

105
00:05:33,660 --> 00:05:35,900
It's like something the dining hall serves.

106
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:38,580
That probably does taste exactly like chicken meat.

107
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:40,940
I heard snake meat tastes like chicken, too.

108
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:43,580
I'm sorry.

109
00:05:43,860 --> 00:05:45,460
I'll cast that healing spell now.

110
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:46,260
Oops.

111
00:05:46,260 --> 00:05:47,000
Now I'm ready.

112
00:05:48,420 --> 00:05:49,900
That was so good.

113
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:52,520
It's the first decent meal I've had in the dungeon.

114
00:05:55,440 --> 00:06:01,060
Um, she and I, we've been trying for three months to get past this part of the dungeon.

115
00:06:01,780 --> 00:06:04,620
But the same monsters wipe us out every single time.

116
00:06:04,620 --> 00:06:10,060
So we thought, since you eat monsters, you could tell us how to get stronger so we can, too.

117
00:06:10,660 --> 00:06:11,360
Oh, sure.

118
00:06:11,360 --> 00:06:14,220
Um, first, improve your eating habits.

119
00:06:14,220 --> 00:06:16,240
Second, get enough sleep.

120
00:06:16,680 --> 00:06:17,920
Third, exercise.

121
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:20,320
Follow these three rules.

122
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:23,080
And in no time, you will get stronger.

123
00:06:23,080 --> 00:06:27,580
What an amazing group of adventurers.

124
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:28,820
I want to be like them.

125
00:06:28,820 --> 00:06:37,800
I don't think we're going to make it to the third floor today.

126
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:40,540
We spent too much time cooking that monster.

127
00:06:40,540 --> 00:06:44,400
They say red dragons wake up once a month to do their hunting.

128
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:47,860
That means the one we ran into had just woken up.

129
00:06:48,060 --> 00:06:50,680
So it's probably asleep now, because its belly's full.

130
00:06:51,100 --> 00:06:54,160
I'd like to find it before it finishes digesting my sister.

131
00:06:54,160 --> 00:06:58,440
Hey, Marcel, would you please pick up the pace a little?

132
00:07:03,080 --> 00:07:04,760
Yeah, my sister's doomed.

133
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:07,320
I'm not slowing us down.

134
00:07:07,560 --> 00:07:08,500
See, nothing's broken.

135
00:07:08,640 --> 00:07:09,300
I can walk.

136
00:07:09,400 --> 00:07:10,100
But you're hurt.

137
00:07:10,640 --> 00:07:11,560
We should take a break.

138
00:07:11,660 --> 00:07:12,540
I'm fine, really.

139
00:07:12,980 --> 00:07:13,800
Oh, please.

140
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:16,260
Acting tough isn't helping one bit.

141
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:20,140
You'll just end up really hurting yourself, and then you'll be a burden on the party.

142
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:23,960
We could take the path outside.

143
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:24,660
It's easier.

144
00:07:24,700 --> 00:07:27,160
Then we'll have to deal with those pesky big bats.

145
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,180
I can take care of big bats with my magic!

146
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:31,100
No, it's fine.

147
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:33,380
We could take the secret path.

148
00:07:33,620 --> 00:07:35,520
There's lots of traps, but few monsters.

149
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:37,440
I have spells that can disarm traps!

150
00:07:37,460 --> 00:07:38,240
Ah, don't bother.

151
00:07:38,440 --> 00:07:40,060
It's faster to let Chilchuk handle them.

152
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:40,800
Oh.

153
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:44,060
If we're taking a path with few monsters on it,

154
00:07:44,200 --> 00:07:46,920
we'll need to stock up on as much food as we can carry.

155
00:07:47,280 --> 00:07:49,180
Won't the leftovers from lunch be enough?

156
00:07:49,380 --> 00:07:50,800
That's mostly meat and eggs.

157
00:07:50,980 --> 00:07:52,520
We need some more vegetables, too.

158
00:07:52,520 --> 00:07:55,400
I know of a spot nearby where mandrakes grow.

159
00:07:55,640 --> 00:07:56,040
Oh!

160
00:07:56,260 --> 00:07:56,740
Oh!

161
00:07:57,140 --> 00:07:59,180
I know all about harvesting mandrakes!

162
00:07:59,400 --> 00:08:00,500
Leave everything to me!

163
00:08:01,820 --> 00:08:04,400
Mandrakes are basic ingredients for magical potions.

164
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:06,980
And I'm the expert here in magic.

165
00:08:07,280 --> 00:08:09,600
So follow my lead and do exactly what I say.

166
00:08:10,060 --> 00:08:12,480
You can't just pull a mandrake out, because it'll scream.

167
00:08:12,720 --> 00:08:15,060
If you hear it, you'll either go insane or worse.

168
00:08:15,260 --> 00:08:15,580
Die.

169
00:08:15,580 --> 00:08:18,960
If we do this the wrong way, we'll lose time.

170
00:08:19,380 --> 00:08:21,180
And poor Fallon is pretty much doomed.

171
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:23,160
So here's how we do this.

172
00:08:24,200 --> 00:08:27,220
First, we need one well-trained dog and one leash.

173
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:28,140
A dog?

174
00:08:28,620 --> 00:08:30,760
Connect the dog to the mandrake with the leash.

175
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:32,600
Call the dog from a safe distance.

176
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:35,860
The dog will run to you and pull the mandrake out.

177
00:08:36,420 --> 00:08:37,780
What happens to the dog?

178
00:08:38,740 --> 00:08:39,660
It dies.

179
00:08:40,020 --> 00:08:40,860
That's horrible.

180
00:08:41,420 --> 00:08:43,960
And where are we supposed to get enough dogs to sacrifice?

181
00:08:44,640 --> 00:08:47,920
Wouldn't you say this plan is not only cruel, but totally inefficient?

182
00:08:48,300 --> 00:08:49,700
But that's what I was taught.

183
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:52,200
Can't we just use a really long piece of rope?

184
00:08:52,360 --> 00:08:52,520
What?

185
00:08:52,660 --> 00:08:55,600
If we used a rope that's long enough so we couldn't hear the scream,

186
00:08:55,900 --> 00:08:57,320
we wouldn't need to kill a dog.

187
00:08:58,280 --> 00:09:00,720
Uh, there's a reason that won't work.

188
00:09:00,900 --> 00:09:03,580
Something about getting the right angle when you pull, or something.

189
00:09:03,580 --> 00:09:12,880
That was scary.

190
00:09:13,340 --> 00:09:14,220
Nice, Marcel.

191
00:09:15,740 --> 00:09:18,520
If you just cut their heads off first, they won't scream.

192
00:09:19,040 --> 00:09:19,860
Makes sense.

193
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:21,780
But that's not the right way!

194
00:09:22,260 --> 00:09:23,900
I've been doing it this way for years.

195
00:09:24,340 --> 00:09:26,000
But this isn't like picking carrots.

196
00:09:26,080 --> 00:09:27,120
It's really dangerous.

197
00:09:27,660 --> 00:09:30,180
Senshi obviously has experience picking them.

198
00:09:30,180 --> 00:09:33,940
And speaking of, have you ever tried to do this like how your book's telling you?

199
00:09:35,020 --> 00:09:37,520
No, I felt sorry for the dog.

200
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:41,160
Well, we don't have a dog, and we're wasting time talking about it.

201
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:43,280
Sorry, but we're doing it Senshi's way.

202
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:48,460
If such an easy method was safe, it would have been in the book and taught at school.

203
00:09:49,260 --> 00:09:53,680
What was I thinking, trying to get them to believe how the book says to pick a mandrake

204
00:09:53,680 --> 00:09:55,500
when I never tried that way before?

205
00:09:57,140 --> 00:09:58,680
That's right, I haven't.

206
00:09:58,680 --> 00:10:00,260
That settles it.

207
00:10:00,620 --> 00:10:02,900
I'll show them the book's method is the right way.

208
00:10:03,300 --> 00:10:04,260
Then they'll see.

209
00:10:04,680 --> 00:10:06,140
I'm so sorry, boys.

210
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:07,080
I forgive you.

211
00:10:07,760 --> 00:10:10,040
Now what can I use instead of a dog?

212
00:10:10,360 --> 00:10:11,180
A monster!

213
00:10:12,120 --> 00:10:14,220
This floor is chock full of big bats.

214
00:10:14,660 --> 00:10:16,580
They should all be sleeping in their nests now.

215
00:10:19,020 --> 00:10:20,520
I hope this works.

216
00:10:20,900 --> 00:10:23,740
The book doesn't say how much strength it takes to yank these out.

217
00:10:23,740 --> 00:10:26,640
You know, it's a big bat nest by all the dung.

218
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:30,260
I never thought Laios' trivia would come in handy.

219
00:10:30,880 --> 00:10:34,120
Now, I need to find somewhere far enough that I can't hear the scream.

220
00:10:34,700 --> 00:10:35,440
Like there!

221
00:10:35,440 --> 00:10:45,100
I can hit it from here easy.

222
00:10:47,440 --> 00:10:50,460
Aere fa'un orbit!

223
00:10:50,460 --> 00:11:01,520
Yes!

224
00:11:04,400 --> 00:11:06,360
Ow! What the heck?

225
00:11:06,920 --> 00:11:07,480
Marcel.

226
00:11:07,480 --> 00:11:20,500
Having experience is important.

227
00:11:21,320 --> 00:11:25,480
For example, now I know using a big bat instead of a dog is a stupid idea.

228
00:11:27,880 --> 00:11:28,300
Marcel!

229
00:11:29,660 --> 00:11:30,920
Is she dead?

230
00:11:30,920 --> 00:11:34,980
Oh, hey there.

231
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:39,940
You used a big bat instead of a dog, huh?

232
00:11:40,240 --> 00:11:41,080
That's stupid.

233
00:11:43,100 --> 00:11:44,460
Hey, can you hear me?

234
00:11:44,760 --> 00:11:45,460
You okay there?

235
00:11:46,240 --> 00:11:48,480
Yeah, I can never feel better.

236
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:49,560
Yeah, right.

237
00:11:50,620 --> 00:11:52,000
She heard the scream.

238
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:54,820
Let's keep talking to her and it'll help clear her head.

239
00:11:56,420 --> 00:11:57,600
Why do you do that?

240
00:11:57,600 --> 00:12:00,780
When I heard my party call me a bird and I freak out.

241
00:12:01,200 --> 00:12:04,940
I do what I did to get back at him and make him grovel out of my foot.

242
00:12:05,240 --> 00:12:06,040
Why, you rotten!

243
00:12:06,220 --> 00:12:07,000
Take it easy.

244
00:12:07,460 --> 00:12:11,980
If I feel like I'm in a way and not helpful, it makes me real sad.

245
00:12:14,860 --> 00:12:17,440
The deeper we get in here, the tougher the monsters.

246
00:12:18,220 --> 00:12:20,300
We need your magic to keep us all safe.

247
00:12:20,960 --> 00:12:24,040
I don't want you wasting your power when we're just three floors down.

248
00:12:24,960 --> 00:12:27,100
We all have strengths and weaknesses.

249
00:12:27,100 --> 00:12:31,060
And when things get crazy, I'm going to need you to be ready.

250
00:12:31,500 --> 00:12:33,960
Till then, we can take care of everything else.

251
00:12:34,920 --> 00:12:36,540
We like to feel useful, too.

252
00:12:39,220 --> 00:12:40,160
Go on, Chillchuck.

253
00:12:40,460 --> 00:12:40,960
Tell her.

254
00:12:40,960 --> 00:12:44,120
I was wrong to say you were a burden.

255
00:12:44,680 --> 00:12:46,560
You've been a big help since day one.

256
00:12:51,560 --> 00:12:53,220
Say it like you mean it, shorty.

257
00:12:53,380 --> 00:12:56,420
I see you've got your lousy attitude back, you little...

258
00:12:56,420 --> 00:12:57,540
This is great.

259
00:12:57,980 --> 00:13:00,340
We snagged ourselves a nice, fat big bat.

260
00:13:00,680 --> 00:13:01,920
How's about we make some omelets?

261
00:13:03,600 --> 00:13:06,200
Are we 100% sure this thing's an egg?

262
00:13:06,400 --> 00:13:08,780
It looks nothing like any egg I've ever seen.

263
00:13:08,940 --> 00:13:10,440
That's what a snake egg looks like.

264
00:13:10,520 --> 00:13:12,680
But only the tail part of it's the snake.

265
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:14,620
All the chickeny parts are the body.

266
00:13:14,780 --> 00:13:15,040
Uh-uh.

267
00:13:15,240 --> 00:13:16,780
The chickeny part's the tail.

268
00:13:16,780 --> 00:13:21,800
Hmm, this one's a different color than the others.

269
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:23,040
You're right.

270
00:13:23,520 --> 00:13:24,980
Oh, that's the one Marsil got.

271
00:13:25,100 --> 00:13:25,940
It still has a head.

272
00:13:26,360 --> 00:13:26,660
Hmm.

273
00:13:27,980 --> 00:13:28,620
Mm-hmm.

274
00:13:29,480 --> 00:13:32,840
First, you chop the mandrakes up and stir-fry them for a bit.

275
00:13:33,260 --> 00:13:34,740
Crack an egg and whip it.

276
00:13:35,360 --> 00:13:38,940
Once it gets all fluffy in the pan, it's ready.

277
00:13:41,660 --> 00:13:43,980
Compared to a chicken egg, this is yellowier.

278
00:13:44,300 --> 00:13:46,420
This one's got the mandrake we picked in it.

279
00:13:46,420 --> 00:13:50,280
And this one's got the mandrake, the elf girl, picked using her big bat method.

280
00:13:50,760 --> 00:13:52,900
Sure, keep rubbing it in, why don't you?

281
00:13:53,160 --> 00:13:53,960
Now, now.

282
00:13:56,300 --> 00:13:56,780
Hmm.

283
00:13:57,340 --> 00:14:00,660
The one with Marsil's mandrake is less bitter and more velvety.

284
00:14:00,940 --> 00:14:01,220
Hmm.

285
00:14:01,520 --> 00:14:02,340
It is good.

286
00:14:02,680 --> 00:14:05,740
I guess it loses whatever toxins it has when it screams.

287
00:14:06,740 --> 00:14:10,700
The basic rule of cooking is the more effort you put in, the better the taste.

288
00:14:11,280 --> 00:14:15,200
I was so hell-bent on being efficient, I totally forgot that fact.

289
00:14:15,200 --> 00:14:17,180
My apologies, Marsil.

290
00:14:17,720 --> 00:14:19,720
You and that cookbook of yours were right.

291
00:14:20,020 --> 00:14:21,660
Hey, this is not a cookbook!

292
00:14:22,240 --> 00:14:24,480
As thanks for pointing out my mistake.

293
00:14:24,980 --> 00:14:27,460
I saved the tastiest parts for you.

294
00:14:27,660 --> 00:14:28,440
Oh, no!

295
00:14:30,200 --> 00:14:31,980
I don't want it!

296
00:14:31,980 --> 00:14:36,400
I'm sick and tired of eating these sticky monsters!

297
00:14:36,400 --> 00:14:42,240
The entrance to the secret path should be around here.

298
00:14:42,420 --> 00:14:43,280
I don't see it.

299
00:14:43,540 --> 00:14:44,840
Lyos, can I borrow your sword?

300
00:14:44,840 --> 00:14:53,340
Here it is.

301
00:14:57,340 --> 00:14:59,260
How the heck did he know?

302
00:15:00,020 --> 00:15:03,220
Chilchuk is a specialist in finding traps and picking locks.

303
00:15:03,980 --> 00:15:04,960
All right, people.

304
00:15:05,220 --> 00:15:08,860
There's tons of traps coming up, so don't make a move until I say so.

305
00:15:08,860 --> 00:15:16,480
One thing I hate more than anything is people getting in my way when I'm trying to work.

306
00:15:19,060 --> 00:15:19,620
Hmm.

307
00:15:26,080 --> 00:15:26,640
Stop!

308
00:15:26,640 --> 00:15:44,700
Only step where I just stepped.

309
00:15:44,700 --> 00:15:55,000
What are you doing?

310
00:15:55,300 --> 00:15:58,020
I clearly said only step where I just stepped.

311
00:15:58,400 --> 00:16:00,700
Why is your foot hanging over on this other tile?

312
00:16:01,020 --> 00:16:02,360
I don't nitpick details.

313
00:16:02,580 --> 00:16:03,800
But the details matter.

314
00:16:04,120 --> 00:16:06,480
If you don't watch your step, you'll kill someone.

315
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:07,840
There are all kinds of traps.

316
00:16:07,980 --> 00:16:10,180
Some of them go off in tandem and some affect each other.

317
00:16:10,180 --> 00:16:13,420
If you trigger one of them, all my calculations go right out the window.

318
00:16:13,780 --> 00:16:16,260
Open your ears and step where I tell you to!

319
00:16:16,500 --> 00:16:16,820
Wait!

320
00:16:19,060 --> 00:16:19,500
Ah!

321
00:16:22,500 --> 00:16:22,940
Hmm.

322
00:16:26,940 --> 00:16:28,960
This room's got all sorts of traps.

323
00:16:29,260 --> 00:16:31,360
This one would crisp you up before you knew it.

324
00:16:31,520 --> 00:16:32,880
Get off that tile already!

325
00:16:33,320 --> 00:16:36,680
Fire traps spray oil and it won't stop till you move your dang foot!

326
00:16:36,820 --> 00:16:37,220
Oil?

327
00:16:37,340 --> 00:16:39,420
The last thing I need is a roasted dwarf!

328
00:16:39,420 --> 00:16:41,100
Roasted, huh?

329
00:16:41,740 --> 00:16:42,920
No, not roasted.

330
00:16:43,160 --> 00:16:43,360
Huh?

331
00:16:43,880 --> 00:16:44,280
Fried.

332
00:16:44,780 --> 00:16:46,300
No, deep fried.

333
00:16:47,240 --> 00:16:47,720
Kakiage!

334
00:16:49,120 --> 00:16:51,140
We're having tempura for lunch today.

335
00:16:51,920 --> 00:16:54,460
You know where the oil for this trap is stored?

336
00:16:54,980 --> 00:16:56,520
It's not used for cooking.

337
00:16:56,900 --> 00:16:58,240
All right, half-foot kid.

338
00:16:58,620 --> 00:17:00,300
You an expert in cooking oil now?

339
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:02,500
I hate it when people call me kid.

340
00:17:02,760 --> 00:17:05,600
Vegetable oil is the most common, and I bet that's what it is.

341
00:17:05,960 --> 00:17:07,820
Either way, I just want to check it for myself.

342
00:17:07,820 --> 00:17:08,640
If not...

343
00:17:08,640 --> 00:17:10,100
No worries, I won't use it.

344
00:17:11,280 --> 00:17:13,260
All right, fine.

345
00:17:13,880 --> 00:17:18,460
But from now on, when we're dealing with traps, you have to swear you'll do exactly what I tell you!

346
00:17:18,660 --> 00:17:21,720
If you don't like following my orders, you can find the oil yourself!

347
00:17:21,720 --> 00:17:26,320
I swear I'll do what you say, and I'll help if you want.

348
00:17:26,460 --> 00:17:27,100
I don't!

349
00:17:27,880 --> 00:17:30,000
Look, we each have a specialty, don't we?

350
00:17:30,540 --> 00:17:31,360
You cook!

351
00:17:31,500 --> 00:17:33,280
I disarm traps and pick locks!

352
00:17:33,820 --> 00:17:34,840
Marcel Magix!

353
00:17:34,840 --> 00:17:37,840
I'd never tell you how to cook something, ever!

354
00:17:38,220 --> 00:17:41,880
So as a professional courtesy, leave me alone so I can do my job!

355
00:17:45,800 --> 00:17:46,840
I knew it.

356
00:17:47,160 --> 00:17:48,480
This is a treasure room.

357
00:17:48,780 --> 00:17:50,760
All those traps were designed to protect it.

358
00:17:53,060 --> 00:17:54,360
It's bolted down.

359
00:17:54,360 --> 00:17:57,520
I should check inside this one.

360
00:17:59,520 --> 00:18:00,800
Give me some space!

361
00:18:02,080 --> 00:18:04,800
He doesn't have to get all snippy about it.

362
00:18:05,180 --> 00:18:07,440
He knows if he screws up, we're dead.

363
00:18:08,380 --> 00:18:10,440
He's just stressing out about it is all.

364
00:18:12,980 --> 00:18:13,700
It's open.

365
00:18:14,420 --> 00:18:14,860
Great.

366
00:18:15,280 --> 00:18:17,100
So how are we gonna get the oil out of it?

367
00:18:17,240 --> 00:18:18,220
I have no idea.

368
00:18:18,220 --> 00:18:24,200
This looks like the spout, and this is the trigger, and if you pull on it, the oil comes out.

369
00:18:24,520 --> 00:18:25,160
I think.

370
00:18:25,720 --> 00:18:29,220
Okay, I'll hold the pot, and you pull the trigger thingy.

371
00:18:29,500 --> 00:18:29,840
Huh?

372
00:18:30,020 --> 00:18:30,980
That's crazy!

373
00:18:31,300 --> 00:18:32,700
It's filled with boiling oil!

374
00:18:33,300 --> 00:18:34,260
Don't worry.

375
00:18:34,620 --> 00:18:35,300
I'm worried!

376
00:18:38,520 --> 00:18:39,920
You asked for it!

377
00:18:42,840 --> 00:18:44,240
Hot, hot, hot, hot!

378
00:18:45,740 --> 00:18:47,400
That's what I was worried about.

379
00:18:48,220 --> 00:18:48,880
You okay?

380
00:18:49,240 --> 00:18:49,500
Ah!

381
00:18:49,840 --> 00:18:50,620
Your thumb!

382
00:18:50,960 --> 00:18:52,440
Hmm, 180 degrees.

383
00:18:52,980 --> 00:18:54,520
Perfect temperature for deep frying.

384
00:18:55,280 --> 00:18:57,620
So, you didn't feel that at all.

385
00:18:57,880 --> 00:18:58,580
That smell.

386
00:18:59,480 --> 00:19:00,260
That taste.

387
00:19:00,540 --> 00:19:01,640
This here is olive oil.

388
00:19:01,940 --> 00:19:02,520
You're kidding.

389
00:19:02,680 --> 00:19:06,160
There used to be tons of olive trees here, and olive oil's easy enough to make.

390
00:19:06,540 --> 00:19:08,320
Huh, figure someone would use it in a trap.

391
00:19:10,200 --> 00:19:11,360
Yeah, I guess.

392
00:19:11,360 --> 00:19:13,280
Now we can deep fry things.

393
00:19:13,880 --> 00:19:14,040
Oh!

394
00:19:14,500 --> 00:19:17,880
We can use that big blade from the trap in the ceiling to chop the meat.

395
00:19:18,220 --> 00:19:18,960
Hold on!

396
00:19:19,100 --> 00:19:21,260
We don't know if that trap will react the same way!

397
00:19:22,520 --> 00:19:27,000
Since I'm no good at dealing with traps, I'll leave trap stuff to the expert.

398
00:19:27,700 --> 00:19:29,320
Do I really have to do it?

399
00:19:29,700 --> 00:19:33,520
Cooking's my specialty, so you have to do whatever I say now.

400
00:19:33,980 --> 00:19:36,080
Those are the terms we agreed to.

401
00:19:36,080 --> 00:19:38,760
I never agreed to that.

402
00:19:47,560 --> 00:19:49,020
What the hell am I doing?

403
00:19:51,160 --> 00:19:52,920
The big bat's all cut up.

404
00:19:53,140 --> 00:19:53,520
Thanks.

405
00:19:55,200 --> 00:19:58,040
Max, get that fire trap going and heat this oil up.

406
00:19:58,040 --> 00:20:00,760
Go on, you got this.

407
00:20:04,420 --> 00:20:08,660
While he's doing that, I'll strip the skin off the meat, then chop the meat into chunks.

408
00:20:09,020 --> 00:20:12,620
After scoring the meat with a knife, I'll rub in some spices and let it rest.

409
00:20:13,160 --> 00:20:16,040
Now's as good a time as any to prepare the mandrake kakiage.

410
00:20:16,940 --> 00:20:19,760
Before you peel its skin off, you should remove its arms and legs.

411
00:20:20,040 --> 00:20:23,040
If they're all tangled, just separate them and they rip right off.

412
00:20:23,700 --> 00:20:28,420
Scrape all the skin off the body and where the arms and legs were, give those places a scrape, too.

413
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:30,100
Next comes the batter.

414
00:20:30,640 --> 00:20:33,820
Mix a basilisk egg with water, then sift in a bit of flour,

415
00:20:34,260 --> 00:20:37,500
give it another mix-in and make damn sure there's no clumps,

416
00:20:37,780 --> 00:20:39,560
then add the chopped mandrake.

417
00:20:39,560 --> 00:20:41,440
How's that fire coming?

418
00:20:41,980 --> 00:20:43,100
I have no idea.

419
00:20:43,480 --> 00:20:46,320
It's not like I ever used a fire trap for cooking before.

420
00:20:48,380 --> 00:20:50,160
Oh, it's just about right.

421
00:20:55,340 --> 00:20:57,480
Once it's fried enough, it doesn't fall apart.

422
00:20:57,640 --> 00:20:58,040
Flip it.

423
00:20:58,300 --> 00:20:58,720
Got that?

424
00:20:59,140 --> 00:21:00,220
Yeah, I got it.

425
00:21:04,440 --> 00:21:05,320
Is it ready?

426
00:21:07,080 --> 00:21:08,420
Damn it, it wasn't ready.

427
00:21:09,560 --> 00:21:10,860
Maybe it needs more heat.

428
00:21:14,220 --> 00:21:15,680
I burned it.

429
00:21:15,980 --> 00:21:16,960
How's it coming along?

430
00:21:17,700 --> 00:21:18,140
Horribly.

431
00:21:18,320 --> 00:21:19,260
Can you just do this?

432
00:21:19,440 --> 00:21:21,180
Fire traps are your specialty.

433
00:21:21,340 --> 00:21:24,300
Traps are my specialty, but deep frying's a cooking thing.

434
00:21:24,880 --> 00:21:26,580
If the heat's too low, it's gooey.

435
00:21:27,020 --> 00:21:28,860
With too much heat, it burns right away.

436
00:21:29,560 --> 00:21:31,780
Then it's not your specialty after all.

437
00:21:32,580 --> 00:21:33,580
This batch looks okay.

438
00:21:36,080 --> 00:21:37,520
I like the color of that.

439
00:21:37,520 --> 00:21:40,180
And it's ready.

440
00:21:40,180 --> 00:21:45,920
I never thought eating deep-fried food was possible in a dungeon.

441
00:21:47,640 --> 00:21:49,280
This came out perfect.

442
00:21:49,680 --> 00:21:50,800
The heat was just right.

443
00:21:51,180 --> 00:21:53,660
When you deep fry, you do it quick at a specific temperature.

444
00:21:54,020 --> 00:21:55,540
That's how you get it nice and crispy.

445
00:21:56,140 --> 00:21:58,580
It's hard to get it right doing it over a campfire.

446
00:21:58,580 --> 00:22:01,220
Not that anyone should use a campfire.

447
00:22:01,920 --> 00:22:05,680
I've been on this floor a bunch and never knew about this useful room.

448
00:22:07,580 --> 00:22:08,560
It's dangerous!

449
00:22:08,920 --> 00:22:10,580
Don't ever come in here without me.

450
00:22:10,700 --> 00:22:11,100
Got it?

451
00:22:11,280 --> 00:22:12,580
You'll end up killing yourself.

452
00:22:13,100 --> 00:22:13,760
I hear you.

453
00:22:14,320 --> 00:22:17,200
And for the record, the way you handle traps is amazing.

454
00:22:17,680 --> 00:22:19,020
You sure know your stuff.

455
00:22:19,580 --> 00:22:22,860
The day we go our separate ways will be a sad day for me.

456
00:22:22,860 --> 00:22:26,100
I can't figure traps like you, that's for sure.

457
00:22:28,400 --> 00:22:29,900
It's not all that hard.

458
00:22:30,540 --> 00:22:34,120
When we get some downtime, I guess I can teach you a little about traps.

459
00:22:34,620 --> 00:22:35,140
Really?

460
00:22:35,640 --> 00:22:37,980
This could go bad in so many ways.

461
00:22:39,320 --> 00:22:42,940
But you did teach me a couple of your cooking skills.

462
00:22:42,940 --> 00:22:49,240
Meat cannot replace bread, and bread cannot replace meat.

463
00:22:49,580 --> 00:22:52,200
But combine them, and they can be quite delicious.

464
00:22:54,700 --> 00:22:57,340
So it is with meals and people.

465
00:22:58,060 --> 00:22:59,720
Delicious in Dungeon.

